1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:24,155
[Host] Welcome to Japanese Daily News. I'm your host. Today is May twenty-fifth. In today's episode, we have one story: the man who created Japan's convenience store culture has passed away at ninety-three. It's a big deal — convenience stores are everywhere in Japan, and it all started with one person. Let's listen.

2
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:40,884
[News] セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。九十三歳でした。死因は心不全です。

3
00:00:41,584 --> 00:00:56,008
[News] 鈴木さんは、千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。セブンイレブンです。

4
00:00:56,707 --> 00:01:08,108
[News] それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。鈴木さんがアメリカのセブンイレブンを日本に持ってきたのです。

5
00:01:08,808 --> 00:01:24,504
[News] 今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。セブンイレブン、ローソン、ファミリーマート。日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

6
00:01:25,203 --> 00:01:45,748
[News] 鈴木さんは「お客様の立場で考えなさい」といつも言っていました。おにぎり、お弁当、ATM、宅配便の受け取り。コンビニのサービスは、鈴木さんのこの考え方から生まれました。

7
00:01:46,447 --> 00:01:58,304
[News] 二千十六年に会長を退任し、名誉顧問になりました。日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

8
00:01:59,004 --> 00:02:02,555
[Host] All right. Let's go through this piece by piece.

9
00:02:03,156 --> 00:02:04,932
[Host] The first sentence:

10
00:02:05,531 --> 00:02:17,867
[Slow] セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。

11
00:02:18,668 --> 00:02:27,740
[Host] Suzuki Toshifumi, honorary advisor of Seven and I Holdings, passed away on May eighteenth. Let's look at the key vocabulary.

12
00:02:28,340 --> 00:02:31,147
[Vocab] 亡くなりました

13
00:02:31,548 --> 00:02:39,587
[Host] Passed away. This is the polite, respectful way to say someone died in Japanese. You'll hear this in news all the time.

14
00:02:40,187 --> 00:02:42,756
[Vocab] 死にました

15
00:02:43,156 --> 00:02:48,915
[Host] The casual way sounds too direct — almost rude when talking about a real person.

16
00:02:49,515 --> 00:02:52,180
[Vocab] 名誉顧問

17
00:02:52,580 --> 00:03:05,108
[Host] Honorary advisor. This is a title given to retired executives. They no longer run the company, but they keep an advisory role. You'll see this title a lot in Japanese corporate news.

18
00:03:05,707 --> 00:03:06,788
[Host] Next:

19
00:03:07,388 --> 00:03:19,891
[Slow] 千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。

20
00:03:20,692 --> 00:03:36,364
[Host] In nineteen seventy-four, he opened the first convenience store in Japan, in Toyosu, Tokyo. That's over fifty years ago. Toyosu, by the way, is now famous for its fish market — the one that replaced Tsukiji.

21
00:03:36,963 --> 00:03:39,963
[Vocab] 第一号店

22
00:03:40,364 --> 00:03:44,948
[Host] Store number one — the very first store. 'Gou' means 'number.'

23
00:03:45,548 --> 00:03:47,395
[Vocab] 店

24
00:03:47,795 --> 00:03:52,259
[Host] And this means 'store.' You'll see this pattern for other firsts too.

25
00:03:52,860 --> 00:03:54,948
[Vocab] 出す

26
00:03:55,348 --> 00:04:05,788
[Host] It literally means 'to put out,' but here it means to open a store. Japanese has many verbs like this where the meaning shifts depending on context. For example:

27
00:04:06,388 --> 00:04:09,460
[Slow] 手紙を出す。

28
00:04:10,259 --> 00:04:14,147
[Host] To send a letter. Same verb, different meaning.

29
00:04:14,747 --> 00:04:17,459
[Host] Then we hear why this was such a big deal:

30
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:24,300
[Slow] それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。

31
00:04:25,100 --> 00:04:37,100
[Host] Until then, there were no convenience stores in Japan. Think about that — no Seven Eleven, no Lawson, no Family Mart. None. He brought the whole concept from America.

32
00:04:37,699 --> 00:04:40,052
[Vocab] それまで

33
00:04:40,451 --> 00:04:50,004
[Host] Until then. A very useful time expression. It points back to a time you already mentioned. You can use it in everyday conversation too:

34
00:04:50,603 --> 00:04:54,660
[Slow] それまで知りませんでした。

35
00:04:55,459 --> 00:04:57,283
[Host] I didn't know until then.

36
00:04:57,884 --> 00:05:01,627
[Host] The news talks about how huge convenience stores are today:

37
00:05:02,228 --> 00:05:09,692
[Slow] 今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。

38
00:05:10,492 --> 00:05:18,675
[Host] Today, there are approximately fifty-six thousand convenience stores in Japan. That's one for roughly every two thousand people.

39
00:05:19,276 --> 00:05:21,315
[Vocab] 約

40
00:05:21,716 --> 00:05:29,420
[Host] Approximately. Short, common, and very useful. You'll hear it constantly in news whenever numbers come up.

41
00:05:30,019 --> 00:05:34,771
[Host] And then a powerful sentence about what convenience stores mean to Japan:

42
00:05:35,372 --> 00:05:41,468
[Slow] 日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

43
00:05:42,267 --> 00:05:45,627
[Host] They became something indispensable to life in Japan.

44
00:05:46,228 --> 00:05:48,747
[Vocab] 欠かせない

45
00:05:49,148 --> 00:06:03,211
[Host] Indispensable, can't do without. This is a strong word. If you've been to Japan, you know it's true — convenience stores are where you pay bills, pick up packages, buy concert tickets, and of course, eat.

46
00:06:03,812 --> 00:06:06,836
[Host] Now, Suzuki's famous philosophy:

47
00:06:07,435 --> 00:06:12,355
[Slow] お客様の立場で考えなさい。

48
00:06:13,156 --> 00:06:20,139
[Host] Think from the customer's perspective. This one sentence explains why Japanese convenience stores are so good.

49
00:06:20,740 --> 00:06:22,947
[Vocab] 立場

50
00:06:23,348 --> 00:06:30,427
[Host] Standpoint, perspective. A very common word in Japanese. You can use it with different particles:

51
00:06:31,028 --> 00:06:35,180
[Slow] 私の立場から言うと。

52
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:40,324
[Host] Speaking from my standpoint. Very useful in discussions.

53
00:06:40,923 --> 00:06:44,307
[Host] The grammar point here is the polite command form.

54
00:06:44,908 --> 00:06:47,091
[Vocab] なさい

55
00:06:47,492 --> 00:06:52,028
[Host] — a polite command form. It's softer than the blunt imperative.

56
00:06:52,627 --> 00:06:55,052
[Vocab] 考えろ

57
00:06:55,451 --> 00:07:02,603
[Host] but it's still top-down. A boss quoting this to employees, a parent to a child — that's the tone.

58
00:07:03,204 --> 00:07:04,908
[Host] And the final line:

59
00:07:05,507 --> 00:07:11,723
[Slow] 日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

60
00:07:12,524 --> 00:07:16,291
[Host] He is called the father of Japan's convenience store industry.

61
00:07:16,891 --> 00:07:19,267
[Vocab] 産業

62
00:07:19,668 --> 00:07:25,596
[Host] Industry. A formal word, mostly used in news and business contexts.

63
00:07:26,196 --> 00:07:29,052
[Vocab] 呼ばれています

64
00:07:29,451 --> 00:07:34,036
[Host] Is being called. This is the passive form of the verb to call.

65
00:07:34,636 --> 00:07:36,651
[Vocab] 呼ぶ

66
00:07:37,052 --> 00:07:49,651
[Host] To call. In the passive form, it becomes 'yobareru,' meaning 'to be called.' Add the ongoing state, and you get 'is being called' — a very natural way to say 'is known as' in Japanese.

67
00:07:50,252 --> 00:07:52,915
[Vocab] 呼ばれている

68
00:07:53,315 --> 00:07:59,411
[Host] This is the ongoing state form. It's a very natural way to say 'is known as' in Japanese.

69
00:08:00,012 --> 00:08:05,603
[Host] Now let's listen to the full story one more time. See how much more you catch.

70
00:08:06,204 --> 00:08:22,331
[News] セブンアンドアイホールディングスの名誉顧問、鈴木敏文さんが、五月十八日に亡くなりました。九十三歳でした。死因は心不全です。

71
00:08:23,031 --> 00:08:37,456
[News] 鈴木さんは、千九百七十四年に、東京の豊洲に、コンビニエンスストアの日本第一号店を出しました。セブンイレブンです。

72
00:08:38,155 --> 00:08:49,556
[News] それまで、日本にコンビニはありませんでした。鈴木さんがアメリカのセブンイレブンを日本に持ってきたのです。

73
00:08:50,255 --> 00:09:05,951
[News] 今、日本にはコンビニが約五万六千店あります。セブンイレブン、ローソン、ファミリーマート。日本の生活に欠かせないものになりました。

74
00:09:06,652 --> 00:09:27,196
[News] 鈴木さんは「お客様の立場で考えなさい」といつも言っていました。おにぎり、お弁当、ATM、宅配便の受け取り。コンビニのサービスは、鈴木さんのこの考え方から生まれました。

75
00:09:27,895 --> 00:09:39,751
[News] 二千十六年に会長を退任し、名誉顧問になりました。日本のコンビニ産業の父と呼ばれています。

76
00:09:40,451 --> 00:09:45,635
[Host] Quick quiz. Three questions in Japanese — try to answer before I do.

77
00:09:46,235 --> 00:09:53,748
[Slow] 鈴木さんは何年にコンビニの第一号店を出しましたか？

78
00:09:54,548 --> 00:10:02,539
[Host] What year did Suzuki open the first convenience store? The answer: nineteen seventy-four. In Toyosu, Tokyo.

79
00:10:03,139 --> 00:10:09,211
[Slow] 今、日本にコンビニは何店ありますか？

80
00:10:10,011 --> 00:10:15,772
[Host] How many convenience stores are there in Japan today? Approximately fifty-six thousand.

81
00:10:16,371 --> 00:10:21,868
[Slow] 鈴木さんはいつも何と言っていましたか？

82
00:10:22,668 --> 00:10:40,716
[Host] What did Suzuki always say? Think from the customer's perspective. Vocabulary recap. Passed away. Honorary advisor. The very first store. Until then. Approximately. Indispensable. Standpoint, perspective. Industry.

83
00:10:41,316 --> 00:10:53,700
[Host] That's today's Japanese Daily News. Full transcript with furigana at podcast.jpnotes.dev. Subscribe on Apple Podcasts or Spotify. See you tomorrow.

84
00:10:54,299 --> 00:10:56,556
[News] また明日。
